     <div class="exercise-info">
        <pre><h1>       Exercise Name: Chin-Up (Underhand Grip Pull-Up)</h1></pre>

<b><u>Overview Video:</u></b><br><br>
This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates Chin-Ups. The video highlights both the biceps (Biceps Brachii) and<br><br> upper back muscles (latissimus dorsi) in bright glowing colors, showing how performing a pull-up variation with an <br><br>underhand (supinated) grip places heavy emphasis on the biceps while pulling your body weight upward.



Exercise Name:Chin-Up (Underhand Grip Pull-Up)

Target Muscles:Biceps (Biceps Brachii) and Upper Back (Latissimus Dorsi)

Secondary Muscles:Brachialis, Forearms, Rear Deltoids, Core

</div>
</div>
<pre>
<h1>
How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1> <u><b><h4>1.Setup:</h4></b></u>Grab a pull-up bar with an underhand grip (palms facing toward you) at about shoulder-width apart. Hang fully with your arms straight and core engaged.
 <u><b><h4>2.Pulling Phase:</h4></b></u>Exhale as you pull your body upward by driving your elbows down toward your sides and squeezing your biceps and back. Continue until your chin clears the bar.
 <u><b><h4>3.Peak Squeeze:Hold for a split second at the top, squeezing your biceps and lats tightly.
 <u><b><h4>4.Lowering Phase:</h4></b></u>Inhale as you slowly lower your body back down in a controlled 2-to-3-second motion until your arms are fully extended.


<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

<b>◉High Bicep Activation:</b>The supinated grip places your biceps in an optimal mechanical advantage, making it one of the most effective compound exercises for arm size and strength.
<b>◉Compound Strength:</b>Works multiple upper-body muscle groups simultaneously, building functional upper-body pulling power.
<b>◉Core & Grip Enhancement:</b>Carrying your entire body weight builds significant grip strength and requires core engagement to keep your body stable.



 <h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy)| 3 – 4 | 8 – 12 | 90 – 120 seconds |
| Endurance| 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 (or bodyweight max) | 60 seconds |
| Strength| 4 – 5 | 4 – 6 (weighted if needed) | 2 – 3 minutes |

Avoid swinging your legs or using momentum (kicking) to lift yourself up—keep the movement smooth and controlled throughout every repetition.
